Publishing duckdb via the openSUSE Build Service (OBS)
======================================================

This document describes the one-time bootstrap and the day-to-day
publish loop for the home:mjgajda/duckdb package on
https://build.opensuse.org/.

Why OBS (and not a Launchpad PPA)
---------------------------------

  - Builds Debian *and* Ubuntu (and openSUSE, RHEL, ...) from one
    source.  Launchpad PPAs are Ubuntu-only.
  - Does not require a GPG key tied to a personal identity; OBS
    signs the produced repositories with its own per-project key.
  - The web UI + osc CLI are scriptable; we drive it from
    debian/scripts/publish.sh.

Trust model
-----------

  - The git repository (github.com/mjgajda/duckdb, branch
    debian/latest) is the canonical source.
  - debian/obs/_service in git is the canonical copy of the OBS
    source-services definition.  The file at the root of the OBS
    package (i.e. <osc-checkout>/_service) is a mirror; publish.sh
    refuses to push if the two have diverged in an unexpected
    direction.
  - CI (.github/workflows/debian-package.yml) verifies that
    debian/latest builds on each supported target, but does NOT
    push to OBS.  Publishing is always a manual `publish.sh --push`
    invocation from the maintainer's laptop, after CI is green.

One-time bootstrap
------------------

1. Create an account at https://build.opensuse.org/.  The username
   becomes your home-project prefix (home:<login>).  This guide
   assumes 'mjgajda'.  Confirm via the email OBS sends; until you
   do, login fails with no diagnostic.

2. Install osc and configure credentials:

       sudo apt install osc
       osc ls                # first run prompts for username/password
                             # and writes ~/.config/osc/oscrc (mode 0600)

   To avoid plaintext passwords on disk, edit ~/.config/osc/oscrc
   and replace the `pass = ...` line under [https://api.opensuse.org]
   with:

       credentials_mgr_class=osc.credentials.KeyringCredentialsManager:keyring.backends.SecretService.Keyring

   Next osc invocation will move the password into the system
   keyring.

3. Create the package and project metadata:

       osc meta pkg -e home:mjgajda duckdb     # opens $EDITOR; paste
                                               # the <package> XML from
                                               # the section below.

       osc meta prj -e home:mjgajda            # opens $EDITOR; paste
                                               # the <project> XML below.

   Submitting either creates the project lazily if it doesn't yet
   exist.

4. Push the canonical _service for the first time:

       cd <duckdb-git-checkout>
       git checkout debian/latest
       debian/scripts/publish.sh --push --wait

   The first run takes longer because OBS has to bootstrap its
   build roots for each target.

Package metadata template (paste into `osc meta pkg -e`)
--------------------------------------------------------

    <package name="duckdb" project="home:mjgajda">
      <title>DuckDB - in-process analytical SQL database</title>
      <description>
        DuckDB is an in-process SQL OLAP database management system
        with a vectorised execution engine, columnar storage and
        broad SQL support.  This package is built from a fork of
        github.com/duckdb/duckdb that adds Debian/Ubuntu packaging
        on the debian/latest branch.
      </description>
      <url>https://github.com/mjgajda/duckdb</url>
    </package>

Project metadata template (paste into `osc meta prj -e`)
--------------------------------------------------------

    <project name="home:mjgajda">
      <title>mjgajda's home project</title>
      <description>Personal package builds.</description>
      <person userid="mjgajda" role="maintainer"/>
      <person userid="mjgajda" role="bugowner"/>

      <repository name="Debian_13">
        <path project="Debian:13" repository="standard"/>
        <arch>x86_64</arch>
      </repository>
      <repository name="Debian_Unstable">
        <path project="Debian:Unstable" repository="standard"/>
        <arch>x86_64</arch>
      </repository>
      <repository name="xUbuntu_22.04">
        <path project="Ubuntu:22.04" repository="universe"/>
        <arch>x86_64</arch>
      </repository>
      <repository name="xUbuntu_24.04">
        <path project="Ubuntu:24.04" repository="universe"/>
        <arch>x86_64</arch>
      </repository>
      <!-- Uncomment once OBS publishes the 26.04 target server-side:
      <repository name="xUbuntu_26.04">
        <path project="Ubuntu:26.04" repository="universe"/>
        <arch>x86_64</arch>
      </repository>
      -->
    </project>

Day-to-day publish loop
-----------------------

1. Sync the upstream branch and rebase debian/latest (see
   debian/README.source).

2. Bump debian/changelog with a new snapshot version.  Commit and
   push to github.com/mjgajda/duckdb so OBS's tar_scm can fetch it.

3. Run CI by pushing.  Wait for .github/workflows/debian-package.yml
   to report green.

4. Sync to OBS and trigger a build:

       debian/scripts/publish.sh --push --wait

   This:
     a. checks the working tree is clean and on debian/latest;
     b. copies debian/obs/_service into the OBS package;
     c. runs `osc commit` (which triggers tar_scm, which clones
        the github mirror at the pinned revision);
     d. blocks until every target in the build matrix finishes;
     e. exits non-zero if any target failed (so you notice).

   Built .deb files appear in your project's repositories pages,
   e.g.:
     https://download.opensuse.org/repositories/home:/mjgajda/Debian_13/

Drift recovery
--------------

If someone edits the OBS package via the web UI (or you forgot to
commit a local _service tweak), use:

       debian/scripts/publish.sh           # show drift
       debian/scripts/publish.sh --pull    # overwrite git from OBS

then commit the result.

Adding new target distributions
-------------------------------

To add (say) Debian 14 once it exists:

  1. `osc meta prj -e home:mjgajda` — add a new <repository>
     block pointing at Debian:14 standard.
  2. No change needed in this git tree (the _service file does not
     enumerate targets — OBS does, in the project meta).
  3. Re-run publish.sh --push to rebuild against the new target.

To stop building for a target: remove its <repository> block from
the project meta.  The previously-built .debs remain available in
the download repo until you also delete them (`osc wipebinaries`).
